# SOLID

Apply SOLID when designing and writing Kotlin/Compose code in this project.

## S — Single Responsibility

- One class/file = one reason to change.
- Split Screen / ViewModel / Reducer / UseCase / Repository. Do not dump loading, mapping, and UI into one type.
- Prefer small use cases over “god” interactors.

## O — Open/Closed

- Extend via new types (new `Intent`, new use case, new repository impl) rather than editing core switch/logic when possible.
- Prefer sealed hierarchies and strategy/impl injection over growing `if/else` chains.

## L — Liskov Substitution

- Implementations of domain interfaces must honor the contract (same expectations for nullability, errors, threading).
- Do not weaken repository/use-case guarantees in “special” impls.

## I — Interface Segregation

- Keep domain repository interfaces focused. Split read/write or unrelated APIs if a client only needs part.
- Avoid fat “AppRepository” that every feature depends on.

## D — Dependency Inversion

- `presentation` and `domain` depend on abstractions; `data` implements them.
- Inject via Koin. High-level modules must not construct low-level IO directly.
- Domain never imports Android framework or data-layer types.

## Practice

- New feature code must respect layer boundaries from the Clean Arch + MVI rule.
- Prefer constructor injection and explicit interfaces at feature boundaries.
- Refactor when a class accumulates unrelated responsibilities — don’t “just add another method.”
